TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R13","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R14","met":"no","where":"Image 1"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The listed RAAN is said to indicate the launch facility's location. RAAN is the inertial orientation of the orbital plane and depends on launch timing and injection geometry, not launch location alone; an epoch is also needed to make the element set reproducible."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"No local time of ascending or descending node is specified. Therefore the claimed consistent illumination and the assertion that descending passes are in sunlight cannot be established; SSO does not by itself make a particular pass direction continuously sunlit."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The proposed 600 km altitude is not supported by payload ground-resolution, swath, or imaging-performance calculations. The report also does not quantify how the imager would cover the reef or meet its monitoring needs."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"The proposed Vega E launch is described as a future, not-yet-built vehicle, and no confirmed launch, rideshare availability, payload interface, or injection compatibility is established."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Mount Pleasant is selected because of its latitude, but the report does not establish that its radio-telescope facilities provide compatible spacecraft TT&C, frequencies, tracking, or operational access. No link budget or data-rate analysis substantiates the claim that contact time is sufficient."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The access table contains a duplicated access entry (entries 14 and 15) and the stated access results do not demonstrate data delivery or communication capacity; access duration alone is insufficient evidence."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The report proposes a drag sail but gives no device sizing, deployment design, lifetime analysis, or disposal-time estimate. It also calls the descent passive while discussing an orbital manoeuvre, despite stating the spacecraft has no thrusters."},{"where":"Introduction","issue":"The bibliography cites Wikipedia for the spaceflight calendar, contrary to the task's requirement not to use Wikipedia as a proper source; references are otherwise largely bare URLs without full bibliographic details."}],"overall":"The report presents a coherent Australian reef-monitoring concept and covers most required mission-design topics, with useful STK evidence. Its design remains preliminary: payload and communications feasibility are unquantified, some orbital and EOL claims are unsupported or inconsistent, and referencing and figure/table labelling need improvement.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The Australian reef-monitoring mission and its rationale are explained in detail, with a clear reason for using one spacecraft and examples of likely customers. The operator is only implied, however, and the users and operational responsibilities are not firmly identified. The rationale is supported by some citations, but the references are not developed to a high standard.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required. This also just allows for simplicity in communications, reduces cost, and requires less time to build and launch."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report addresses launch, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, a ground facility and access results, duration, and an EOL concept. STK outputs and orbit illustrations provide some evidence. However, several key design choices are weakly supported or technically inconsistent: the ground station suitability and communications capability are not demonstrated, the SSO local solar time is unspecified, and the EOL approach is not developed into a workable design. Launch and imaging feasibility are also not demonstrated quantitatively.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Systems Tool Kit 11 (STK) was used to calculate access periods as seen below:"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"A span of five years is a sound estimate of the useful lifespan of the proposed satellite."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion draws together the main mission choices and acknowledges launch-vehicle uncertainty. It is useful but largely summarizes the design rather than evaluating whether the evidence demonstrates mission performance or resolving key uncertainties.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"Thus, launch may need to be adjusted if the rocket is still not constructed by the proposed date."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a recognizable structure, an executive summary, headings, and multiple relevant visuals and tables. Presentation is weakened by unnumbered or inconsistently captioned figures and tables, grammatical and typographical errors, and sparse URL-only references. The bibliography also includes Wikipedia, which the task explicitly does not accept as a proper source.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"DN path for orbits 1-15"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."}]}]}
